INT. ABANDONED CANNERY - SUBLEVEL FREIGHT ELEVATOR - NIGHT

The cage is the size of a small room, riveted iron gone the color of old blood. A single caged bulb swings on its wire. Below the grated floor, black water slaps against pilings, in no rhythm that a tide would keep.

IONA MACREADY, 30s, the surveyor, holds a clipboard against her chest like a shield. Beside her, TEDDY BOLAÑO, 20s, her assistant, shaves the dust off his lantern's glass with his thumb. DR. PRIYA SUNDARAM, 50s, a marine biologist with cropped silver hair and a field vest of many pockets, crouches to put her ear against the iron floor.

Against the far wall, GIDEON FARROW, 70s, the last cannery watchman, leans on a boat hook. Tucked behind his legs, as if he did not want to be seen being there, is NICO, 8, his grandson, in a yellow slicker two sizes too big.

IONA
How many floors down?

GIDEON
Four. Maybe five.

IONA
The plans say three.

GIDEON
The plans say a lot of things.

The cage lurches. The bulb swings wild; shadows leap and tangle on the walls. Nico grabs his grandfather's coat.

PRIYA
(standing)
Something is moving the water. Not the tide.

TEDDY
Maybe a pipe.

PRIYA
A pipe doesn't breathe.

They look at her. Water below the floor pulls back, a long slow draw, as if the sea were inhaling. The grating rattles. All five freeze.

NICO
Granddad. It's in the elevator.

GIDEON
Hush.

NICO
It's under my feet.

The floor grating bulges upward. Pale fingers, too many, slip through the slots. Teddy screams, drops the lantern. Iona yanks Nico into her arms while Gideon swings the boat hook down onto the grate with a hollow clang. The fingers withdraw, quick as eels.

Dark. A pulse of lamplight, then dark again. The bulb flickers back on.

Everyone is breathing hard. Nobody speaks. Then:

PRIYA
It didn't want us. It wanted the light.

IONA
How do you know?

PRIYA
It stopped when the lantern fell.

TEDDY
Then we stay in the dark.

GIDEON
We tried that. Nineteen sixty-one. The lamp went out and the crew all rode down singing. Nobody rode back.

NICO
Why were they singing?

GIDEON
Because it asked them nicely.

The cage drops. Not a lurch this time: a plunge, a controlled, fast, deliberate fall. Iona clutches the clipboard. Priya braces against the wall. Teddy sinks down with his head between his knees. Gideon lifts the hook and holds it level like a lance.

The fall stops. A soft thud. The iron door slides open on its own.

Beyond: a vaulted hall of wet stone, lit by something green and far off. A hum rises, many voices, tuneless, patient, familiar. Nico, in Iona's arms, tilts his head.

NICO
Mama?

IONA
No. No, baby. No.

She turns his face into her shoulder. The hum grows warmer, kinder.

TEDDY
(whispering)
I can hear my dad.

PRIYA
Everyone, hold your breath. If you can't hear it, it can't use you.

They hold. The hum swells to a sweet pitch, then breaks into a single laugh. Gideon lowers the boat hook.

GIDEON
(quiet)
It's laughing at the rule.

The elevator door starts to close. The five of them stare at the narrowing gap, not one of them moving toward it, and not one of them moving away.
